// c35_effecting_let_secret: an effecting fn whose single `let` is annotated
// `secret i64` — the shape that used to make `snc build` return a RAW POINTER.
//
// WHAT IT PINS (ADR 0072). An effecting fn returns a `*mut SentinelKont`, not its
// declared type. `widened` and `exact` below are the c35c "let-shape": one `let` bound
// to a kont producer, then a pure tail, lowered by reifying the evaluation frame into
// a resumer fn the runtime re-enters with the resumed value.
//
// Adding ONE qualifier used to break it. `detect_let_shape` required the let's
// annotated type to be exactly `i64`, and `tail_produces_kont` could not see through
// the `secret` widen — so `let s: secret i64 = ...` matched no shape and fell through
// to the generic straight-line emitter, which lowered the callee as though it returned
// `i64`. The `Kont*` was stored into an i64 slot and returned as the answer: the exit
// status was a heap address that VARIED RUN TO RUN with ASLR (-1341102038, 783502784,
// -453424688 on three runs), with no diagnostic at any stage.
//
// It survived because `llvm-as` catches it but `module.verify()` cannot: under LLVM 18
// opaque pointers, storing a `ptr` into an `i64` alloca is well-formed. So the text
// oracle emitted something invalid while the shipped compiler emitted something that
// linked, ran, and lied.
//
// `secret i64` is ADMITTED rather than merely refused because it IS the seam's i64:
// `secret T` lowers identically to `T` (ADR 0019 D12) and the widen is value-level
// identity (ADR 0051 A2). Everything that does not fit that one i64 — `?i64` at 16
// bytes, `bool`/`i32`/`u8` narrower, aggregates — is refused instead, pinned by the
// `c35_effecting_*` fixtures in tests/ui.
//
// THE THREE CELLS, each a different way to reach the boundary:
//   `widened`  — RHS is an `i64`-returning call under a `secret i64` annotation, so a
//                `WidenToSecret` node sits between the let and the call. This is the
//                half that defeated `tail_produces_kont`.
//   `exact`    — RHS already returns `secret i64`, so there is NO widen node at all.
//                This is the half that defeated the `ty == I64` guard on its own, and
//                it is why fixing only the widen would have left the bug reachable.
//   `gives_secret` — a let-shape that CAPTURES a `secret i64` param, exercising the
//                other half of the boundary: captures cross the same `i64[N]` seam and
//                were checked by nothing before (a narrower one is read out of bounds,
//                see the `narrow_capture` ui fixture).
//
// ⚠ TWO NEIGHBOURING scg GAPS THIS FIXTURE IS SHAPED TO AVOID, both pre-existing and
// unrelated to ADR 0072, both tracked separately. Change the shapes below and the
// codegen differential will go red for reasons that have nothing to do with this fix:
//   * No `perform` may sit under a `secret` expectation — `let z: secret i64 = perform
//     …` or `-> secret i64 { perform … }`. scg's `Expr::Perform` arm does not emit the
//     widen wrapper (oracle `(widen-secret (perform …))`, scg the bare node), and for
//     the bare-perform TAIL scg skips the embedded-perform reification entirely
//     (oracle 2 resumers, scg 0). Hence `gives_secret` gets its secrecy from the
//     `base + z` operand widen, which IS mirrored (ADR 0051 A1), not from the perform.
//   * EVERY param of a let-shape fn must be referenced in its TAIL. scg captures all
//     params in declaration order while the oracle captures the tail's free vars in
//     first-reference order, so an unused param makes scg over-capture (a spurious
//     `sentinel_alloc` + GEP/store the oracle omits). That is why `exact`'s tail is
//     `declassify(t + base)` and not `declassify(t)`.
//
// 20 + 22 = 42.

effect Io {
    read() -> i64;
}

fn do_work() -> i64 ! { Io } {
    perform Io.read()
}

// A `secret i64`-returning effecting fn — itself a let-shape, and the one that
// captures a `secret i64` param across the frame.
fn gives_secret(base: secret i64) -> secret i64 ! { Io } {
    let z: i64 = perform Io.read();
    base + z
}

// The widen path: an `i64`-returning callee under a `secret i64` annotation.
fn widened() -> i64 ! { Io } {
    let s: secret i64 = do_work();
    declassify(s) - 2
}

// The exact-match path: no widen node anywhere — the let type simply is not `i64`.
fn exact(base: secret i64) -> i64 ! { Io } {
    let t: secret i64 = gives_secret(base);
    declassify(t + base)
}

fn main() -> i64 {
    let b: secret i64 = 0;
    let x: i64 = handle widened() with {
        Io.read(k) => k(22)
    };
    let y: i64 = handle exact(b) with {
        Io.read(k) => k(22)
    };
    x + y
}
